📖 What is DNS?

DNS (Domain Name System) is a distributed hierarchical system that converts a Domain Name into an IP Address.

Humans can easily remember names such as:

google.com
amazon.in
github.com

But computers communicate using IP Addresses.

DNS acts as a translator between humans and computers.

🤔 Why Do We Need DNS?

Imagine if websites did not have names.

Instead of typing:

google.com

You would have to remember:

142.250.183.78

Now imagine remembering the IP address of every website you visit.

It would be almost impossible.

DNS solves this problem by allowing users to use Domain Names, while computers continue communicating using IP Addresses.

🌍 Real-Life Example

Think about your mobile contacts.

You save:

Rahul

instead of

+91-9876543210

When you tap Rahul, your phone internally finds the phone number.

Similarly,

google.com
        ↓
142.250.xxx.xxx

DNS converts the name into an IP address.

🌐 What is a Domain Name?

A Domain Name is the human-readable address of a website.

Examples
google.com
amazon.in
github.com
openai.com
linkedin.com

Humans remember names.

Computers remember numbers.

🌍 What is an IP Address?

An IP Address is a unique numerical address assigned to every device connected to a network.

Just like every house has a postal address,

every server on the Internet has an IP address.

Example:

142.250.xxx.xxx

Without an IP Address,

devices cannot communicate over the Internet.

🔍 DNS Resolution Process

Suppose you type:

https://google.com

Your browser follows a series of steps to find Google's IP Address.

User
  │
  ▼
Browser
  │
  ▼
Browser Cache
  │
  ▼
OS Cache
  │
  ▼
Recursive DNS Resolver
  │
  ▼
Root DNS Server
  │
  ▼
TLD Server (.com)
  │
  ▼
Authoritative DNS Server
  │
  ▼
IP Address Returned

After receiving the IP Address,

the browser starts building the network connection.

🧠 Step 1 — Browser Cache

The browser first checks whether it already knows the IP Address.

Example:

Chrome Cache

google.com

↓

142.250.xxx.xxx

If found,

the browser immediately uses the cached IP.

No DNS lookup is required.

Benefits
Faster browsing
Less network traffic
Better performance
💻 Step 2 — Operating System Cache

If the browser cache doesn't contain the IP,

the browser asks the Operating System.

Chrome

↓

Windows / Linux / macOS

↓

DNS Cache

If the operating system already has the IP,

it returns it immediately.

Otherwise,

the next step begins.

🌐 Step 3 — Recursive DNS Resolver

If neither the Browser nor the Operating System knows the IP Address,

the request is sent to a Recursive DNS Resolver.

Usually provided by:

Jio
Airtel
BSNL

Public DNS services:

Google DNS (8.8.8.8)
Cloudflare DNS (1.1.1.1)

The Recursive Resolver performs the complete DNS lookup on behalf of the browser.

🌍 Step 4 — Root DNS Server

The Root DNS Server does not know the website's IP Address.

Instead,

it tells the resolver which Top-Level Domain (TLD) server should be contacted.

Example:

google.com

↓

.com TLD Server
🌍 Step 5 — TLD (Top-Level Domain) Server

A Top-Level Domain (TLD) is the last part of a domain name.

Examples:

.com
.in
.org
.net
.edu

The TLD Server tells the resolver which Authoritative DNS Server manages the requested domain.

🌍 Step 6 — Authoritative DNS Server

The Authoritative DNS Server stores the actual DNS records.

Example:

google.com

↓

142.250.xxx.xxx

The server returns the correct IP Address to the resolver.

⏳ What is TTL (Time To Live)?

TTL defines how long a DNS record should remain in the cache before a fresh lookup is performed.

Example:

TTL = 300 Seconds

This means the browser can reuse the cached IP Address for the next 300 seconds.

Benefits
Faster website loading
Reduced DNS traffic
Better performance
📄 Common DNS Record Types
Record	Purpose
A	Maps Domain → IPv4 Address
AAAA	Maps Domain → IPv6 Address
CNAME	Alias of another domain
MX	Mail Server
TXT	Verification & Security
NS	Name Server Information
🏢 Real World Example

Suppose you open:

https://amazon.in

The DNS lookup happens in the following order:

Browser Cache
        │
        ▼
OS Cache
        │
        ▼
Recursive Resolver
        │
        ▼
Root DNS
        │
        ▼
.in TLD Server
        │
        ▼
Amazon Authoritative DNS
        │
        ▼
Amazon IP Address

Only after receiving the IP Address does the browser establish the TCP connection.

