🧠 Key Takeaways
DNS converts Domain Names into IP Addresses.
Computers communicate using IP Addresses.
Browser checks its cache before performing DNS lookup.
The Operating System also maintains a DNS cache.
Recursive Resolver performs the complete lookup.
Root DNS points to the correct TLD Server.
TLD Server points to the Authoritative DNS Server.
Authoritative DNS Server returns the actual IP Address.
TTL improves performance by reducing repeated DNS lookups.
❌ Common Mistakes
Mistake 1

Thinking DNS is a single server.

Reality: DNS is a distributed hierarchical system.

Mistake 2

Thinking the browser directly contacts the Root DNS Server.

Reality: The browser first checks Browser Cache, OS Cache, and then contacts the Recursive Resolver.

Mistake 3

Thinking DNS stores website content.

Reality: DNS stores only domain-related records.

